ht not fit \"loop dependencies\" transformation. The problem statement says \"You can modify the program in any way you see fit\", so we could change algorithm, but the goal is specifically about SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies.\n\nGiven the baseline uses snake traversal, maybe we can replace it with a simpler row-major sliding window that computes each submatrix sum by updating from previous row's sums. That would allow vectorized updates of entire rows using NEON or auto-vectorization. For example:\n- Compute first SxS sum for top-left.\n- Then slide horizontally: subtract left column, add right column (each operation can be done with SIMD on the whole column? But columns are not contiguous in memory; matrix is row-major so accessing a column is strided and harder to vectorize. However, we could transpose or use a different storage order.\n\nBetter approach: Use prefix sums (integral image) to compute any submatrix sum in O(1). That would eliminate the sliding window loops entirely and allow trivial parallelization/vectorization of the max search? But then the main work is computing prefix sums which can be done with SIMD. However, that changes algorithm significantly.\n\nGiven the constraints: N < 8000, S up to N-1. The baseline O(S*N^2) might be okay but we want speedup. Prefix sum approach would be O(N^2) for building integral image and then O((N-S+1)^2) for checking all submatrices (which is also O(N^2)). That could be faster because the inner loop becomes simple arithmetic without conditionals.\n\nBut the goal specifically mentions \"loop dependencies\" – perhaps we need to ensure loops are free of dependencies so they can be vectorized. The current code has a while loop with state variable dir and i,j updates that create dependencies across iterations (i, j, sum). That prevents parallelization/vectorization at outer level.\n\nWe could rewrite the algorithm as two nested for-loops over top-left corners: `for(int i=0; i<=n-s; ++i) for(int j=0; j<=n-s;